{"name":"Blue Danube","hex":"#0094bb","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Blue Danube","blocks":[{"text":"\"The Blue Danube\" is a waltz by the Austrian composer Johann Strauss II, composed in 1866. Originally performed on 15 February 1867 at a concert of the Wiener Männergesang-Verein, it has been one of the most consistently popular pieces of music in the classical repertoire. Its initial performance was considered only a mild success, however, and Strauss is reputed to have said, \"The devil take the waltz, my only regret is for the coda—I wish that had been a success!\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Donauwalzer_Spina_01.jpg/330px-Donauwalzer_Spina_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blue_Danube","wikidata":"Q482621"},{"title":"The Blue Danube (1926 film)","blocks":[{"text":"The Blue Danube is a 1926 German silent romance film directed by Frederic Zelnik and starring Harry Liedtke, Lya Mara and Hans Junkermann. The film has been described as a paean to Austria. It was shot at the Staaken Studios in Berlin. The film's art direction was by Andrej Andrejew and Jacek Rotmil who designed the sets."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/32/The_Blue_Danube_%281926_film%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blue_Danube_(1926_film)","wikidata":"Q14954286"},{"title":"Blue Danube (nuclear weapon)","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Danube was the first operational British nuclear weapon. It also went by a variety of other names, including Smallboy, the Mk.1 Atom Bomb, Special Bomb and OR.1001, a reference to the Operational Requirement it was built to fill."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/9/90/Blue_Danube_Bomb.jpg/330px-Blue_Danube_Bomb.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Danube_(nuclear_weapon)","wikidata":"Q4929038"},{"title":"Danube","blocks":[{"text":"The Danube is a river in Europe, the second-longest after the Volga. It flows through Central and Southeastern Europe, from the Black Forest of Germany south through the Danube Delta in Romania into the Black Sea. A large and historically important river, it was once a frontier of the Roman Empire. In the 21st century, it connects ten European countries, running through their territories or marking a border. Originating in Germany, the Danube flows southeast for 2,850 km (1,770 mi), passing through or bordering Austria, Slovakia, Hungary, Croatia, Serbia, Romania, Bulgaria, Moldova, and Ukraine. Among the many cities on the river are four national capitals: Vienna, Bratislava, Budapest, and Belgrade. Its drainage basin amounts to 817,000 km2 (315,000 sq mi) and extends into nine more countries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/View_from_Gell%C3%A9rt_Hill_to_the_Danube%2C_Hungary_-_Budapest_%2828493220635%29.jpg/330px-View_from_Gell%C3%A9rt_Hill_to_the_Danube%2C_Hungary_-_Budapest_%2828493220635%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danube","wikidata":"Q1653"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/Bluedanubewaltz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/2b/Bluedanubewaltz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"First few bars of The Blue Danube waltz, signed by the composer","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABluedanubewaltz.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Operation_Buffalo-Marcoo.jpg/330px-Operation_Buffalo-Marcoo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Operation_Buffalo-Marcoo.jpg/330px-Operation_Buffalo-Marcoo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Explosion of a Blue Danube warhead (codenamed Buffalo R2/Marcoo, fired on 4 October 1956) during the British nuclear tests at Maralinga","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOperation_Buffalo-Marcoo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Blue_danube_nuclear_weapons.jpg/500px-Blue_danube_nuclear_weapons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Blue_danube_nuclear_weapons.jpg/1280px-Blue_danube_nuclear_weapons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Blue_danube_nuclear_weapons.jpg/500px-Blue_danube_nuclear_weapons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Replica Blue Danube nuclear weapon at Gorse Industrial Estate","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_danube_nuclear_weapons.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Danube_basin.png/500px-Danube_basin.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Danube_basin.png/1280px-Danube_basin.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 